## ACT 1: THE RACE BEGINS

Chaos erupted.

Thirty vehicles launched simultaneously, tires screaming, engines roaring, dust exploding into the air. Immediate collisions—two cars clipped each other, spun out, crashed into the crowd barriers. Screams. Cheers.

The Caldera Death Run didn't fuck around.

Dax punched the accelerator, combat sedan surging forward. Heavy, armored, built to tank damage. Not the fastest, but it could survive what others couldn't.

Kai tracked the battlefield through his HUD:

- **Elena/Ashira**: Racing car already ahead, weaving through traffic with surgical precision
- **Silas**: Solo racer, aggressive driving, forcing competitors off-road
- **Cray's convoy**: Maintaining formation, professional spacing, ignoring the chaos

"Elena, status?" Kai called over comms.

"Clear. Taking lead position. I'll scout ahead, mark obstacles."

"Copy."

The terrain shifted—sand dunes, rock formations, abandoned structures from old nomad settlements. The circuit was a 30km loop through Caldera's most treacherous ground.

Other racers opened fire. Gunshots cracked through engine noise. Someone's vehicle exploded, flipped, tumbled across the sand.

Dax swerved around the wreckage. "This is exactly why I left."

Ahead, Cray's convoy began evasive maneuvers. They'd spotted the pursuit.

Silas's voice crackled through comms: "Rear guard vehicle breaking formation. I'm on it."

Kai watched through his HUD as Silas's car slammed into Cray's trailing vehicle, forcing it off-course. The security vehicle tried to recover, lost traction, rolled.

One down.

Elena: "They're accelerating. Two vehicles left. We need to split them."

Ashira: "I'll handle it."

The racing car pulled alongside Cray's remaining escort vehicle. Ashira leaned out, tech pistol raised—smart-targeting engaged. Three shots. Front tire, rear tire, engine block.

The escort vehicle swerved violently, lost control, crashed into a rock formation.

Two down.

Cray was alone now. One vehicle. Cornered prey.

"Kai," Dax said. "We've got company."

Kai's HUD flashed red. New contact. Coming fast.

A muscle car—modified, aggressive, nomad markings.

Kade.

---

## ACT 2: KADE'S AMBUSH

The muscle car came out of nowhere, emerging from a side route Dax hadn't seen. Slammed into the combat sedan's rear quarter panel with bone-jarring force.

Dax cursed, wrestled the wheel. "That's—"

"KADE!" The voice over their comms—hacked frequency. "You ran, Dax! You left us to DIE!"

Another ram. Harder this time. The sedan's frame groaned.

Kai grabbed the oh-shit handle. "That's your friend?!"

"Was my friend!"

Kade's muscle car pulled alongside, driver's window down. Kai caught a glimpse—mid-thirties, nomad tattoos, Caldera Pack markings. Eyes full of rage.

"You abandoned your clan!" Kade screamed over the engine roar. "Your father died because YOU RAN!"

Dax's jaw clenched. "I followed orders! He told me to—"

"TO SAVE YOURSELF! While we bled out in the sand!"

Kade swerved, trying to force them off-road into dangerous terrain. Dax countered, but barely.

Kai drew his pistol. "Dax, I'm taking the shot."

"No! Don't—he's—"

"Trying to kill us. That's what he is."

Kade opened fire first. Bullets sparked off the sedan's armored plating. One shattered the passenger window. Kai ducked, glass raining down.

"Fuck this." Kai leaned out, returned fire. Three shots at the muscle car's tires.

Kade swerved, evaded. Good driver. Too good.

The chase intensified. Kade knew every dune, every rock formation, every shortcut. This was his home. He drove like the land itself was helping him.

Dax struggled to keep up. "I can't shake him!"

"Then don't. Lead him somewhere open. Give Elena a shot."

Dax keyed comms. "Elena, Ashira—we're pinned down. Kade's on us."

Elena: "Copy. Rerouting. Sixty seconds."

Sixty seconds. They had to survive sixty seconds.

Kade rammed them again. The sedan's rear axle groaned. Another hit like that and they'd lose mobility.

Kai fired again. Hit the muscle car's side mirror, nothing critical.

Kade laughed over comms. "You think you can stop me?! I've been waiting THREE YEARS for this!"

Dax's voice cracked. "Kade, please! It wasn't my fault!"

"You're alive. They're not. That's all the fault I need."

Another ram. The sedan's tire pressure warning flashed.

Then—salvation.

Elena's racing car appeared from a parallel route, cutting across the terrain at impossible speed. Ashira in the passenger seat, tech SMG raised.

Precision fire. Controlled bursts. Ashira didn't aim for Kade—aimed for the engine.

Rounds punched through the muscle car's hood. Steam erupted. Engine sputtering.

Kade's car lost power, started to slow.

"NO!" His scream over comms. "NOT LIKE THIS!"

The muscle car coasted, died, stopped in the middle of the wasteland.

Dax wanted to stop. Kai saw it in his posture—the guilt, the need to explain, to make peace.

"Keep driving," Kai said.

"But—"

"Cray's getting away. We finish the job."

Dax hesitated. Then pressed the accelerator.

In the rearview, Kade climbed out of his dead vehicle, screaming curses they couldn't hear anymore.

Dax's hands shook on the wheel.

Kai put a hand on his shoulder. "You chose the crew. That's what matters."

"I left him again."

"You chose us. Don't apologize for that."

Dax said nothing. Just drove.

Behind them, Kade became a speck in the dust.

